\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e10.para.7-17-4}7.17.4. When \(S\) is regular at \(s\) and \(t\) is maximal, one can construct \(S'\) by blowing up \(s\) in the localization of \(S\) at \(s\), and localizing at the generic point of the fiber of \(s\) in the blow-up. One then obtains for \(k(s')\) a \uline{pure extension} of \(k(s)\). Now it is not difficult to show that, when \(k'\) is a pure extension of a field \(k\), for every noetherian scheme \(T\) over \(k\) the canonical homomorphism \(K_\bullet(T)\to K_\bullet(T\otimes_k k')\) is an isomorphism, \var{by representing \(K'\) as}{by representing \(k'\) as}{EN-CONTRIB-X-052} the inductive limit of the coordinate rings of affine open subsets of schemes of the form \(\Spec k[T_1,\ldots,T_n]\), and using (IX 1.6). Thus one concludes that (7.17.1) can be regarded as a canonical homomorphism
