i.e. makes \(\operatorname{Coker}u\) zero. Since \var{the formation of \(\operatorname{Coker}u\) commutes with the base diagram}{the formation of \(\operatorname{Coker}u\) commutes with base change}{EN-CONTRIB-XII-033}, we see that it is represented by the open subset \(S-\lambda(\operatorname{supp}(\operatorname{Coker}u))\) of \(S\). We may therefore suppose that \(u\) is already an epimorphism. But, now using the hypothesis that \(G\) is \(S\)-flat, we find that the formation of \(\operatorname{Ker}u\) commutes with base change, and consequently the functor under consideration is represented by the open subset \(S-\lambda(\operatorname{supp}(\operatorname{Ker}u))\) of \(S\).

