ment of $K$ such that $\lambda^iN=0$ for sufficiently large $i$. We can then, for every $x\in K$ and every integer $p\geq1$, consider the elements $\lambda^p(N,x)$, which are expressed by universal polynomials with integer coefficients in the $\lambda^iN$ and the $\lambda^jx$. We have $\lambda^1(N,x)=x$ and we have formula (1.32) \emph{in} $K$. To express the properties of the operations $x\mapsto\lambda^p(N,x)$, it is convenient to introduce a new $\lambda$-ring denoted $K_N$. As a commutative ring, $K_N$ is obtained by adjoining a unit to the ring whose underlying additive group is $K$ and whose multiplication is given by
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-34}\begin{equation}\tag{1.34}
x_Ny=xy\mu\qquad (\mu=\lambda_{-1}(N)).
\end{equation}
The map $\lambda_t$ from $K_N$ to $K_N[[t]]$ is defined by:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-35}\begin{equation}\tag{1.35}
\lambda_t(n,x)=(1+t)^n\sum_{p\geq0}\lambda^p(N,x)t^p,
