where we have put $\lambda^0(N,x)=(1,0)$ (the unit element of $K_N$). I say that, \emph{equipped with these operations, $K_N$ is a special $\lambda$-ring}. We therefore have to verify the formulas $\lambda_t(X+Y)=\lambda_t(X)\lambda_t(Y)$, $\lambda_t(XY)=\lambda_t(X).\lambda_t(Y)$, and $\lambda_t(\lambda^i(X))=\lambda^i(\lambda_t(X))$ for $X,Y\in K_N$. We can restrict ourselves to verifying these formulas when $X$ and $Y$ are in $K$, and we find that it suffices to verify them when $K$ is the free special $\lambda$-ring generated by $X,Y$ and $N$ subject to the relations $\lambda^iN=0$ for $i>q$. But the group homomorphism
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-36}\begin{equation}\tag{1.36}
K_N\longrightarrow K,
\end{equation}
which takes unit to unit and on $K$ reduces to the homomorphism $x\longrightarrow x\mu$, is a homomorphism compatible with the ring structures and the maps $\lambda^i$, as we verify immediately. In the present case,
